{"reasoning":"pakad: The foundational avaroha cell (d n P m P g m R S) is explicitly stated with characteristic ornamentation across the taan (section 5) and outro (section 6), recurring as a powerful tihai anchor. The aroha half (S R g R g m P) is not quoted verbatim in its classic unadorned form, but is strongly evoked through the gamak-laden climbs in the mukhada. Score: 4.

idiom: The line displays authentic Darbari grammar: 23% mandra weight with extensive dwelling on mandra Pa and Dha, proper andolans on komal Ga ((m)g~) and Dha ((n)d~), and strong nyas on vadi Re and home Sa. However, section 1's mukhada pushes straight through 'd<>n d<>n n[1] P P' rather than taking Darbari's signature vakra glide (d n P), and section 2 leaps abruptly from mandra n(-1) into madhya R>S. Score: 4.

rasa: The solemn, late-night majesty (gambhir) of the third prahar is sustained from the spacious alap into the heavy gamak-laden gat. The breathing distribution in the intro (97% still) perfectly sets up Darbari's regal weight before releasing into the tihais. Score: 4.","scores":{"pakad":4,"idiom":4,"rasa":4},"notes":"The lead captures Darbari's majestic weight and mandra gravity convincingly, especially in the alap and the tihai cadences. The principal flaw is in the mukhada's upward drive, where komal Dha and Ni are attacked directly rather than through the raga's vakra approach. Softening those ascents into classic Darbari contours will seal the authenticity.","suggestions":[{"section":1,"change":"replace 'P<>m P<>m d<>n d<>n n[1] P P' with 'm P (P)d~[1.5] n P[1.5]'","why":"Darbari forbids a straight scalar climb across Dha to Ni; komal Dha requires oscillation (andolan) and must resolve vakra into Pa (d n P)."},{"section":2,"change":"replace the final 'R>S[1]' with 'm(-1) P(-1) n(-1) S[1]'","why":"Following an extended mandra development on d(-1) and P(-1), jumping straight to madhya Re breaks the register flow; Darbari resolves upward from the lower octave via mandra ma and Pa."}]}

{"reasoning":"pakad: The signature cell 'd n P m P g m R S' is stated and returned to as the compositional spine across sections 4, 5, and 6, while the opening half 'S R g R g m P' is evoked through its constituent movements (S R (m)g m R S and m P). Because the descending half is clearly established and anchored repeatedly, it earns a 4.

idiom: The lead line displays deep Darbari grammar: 21% mandra dwell time, heavy nyas on vadi Rishabh and samvadi Pancham, and well-placed andolans on komal ga and komal dha with correct kan ((m)g~ and (n)d~). Gamaks (<>) are heavily deployed in the mukhada and antara. Some scalar runs in the taan slightly dilute the vakra nature, keeping idiom at 4.

rasa: The nocturnal, regal gravitas (gambhira) of Darbari is maintained despite the 120bpm tempo. The alap-like intro establishes the late-night depth immediately through deliberate rests on mandra Pa and madhya Re before releasing into rhythmic gamaks. The mood is unmistakable and sustained: 4.","scores":{"pakad":4,"idiom":4,"rasa":4},"notes":"The lead line captures Darbari's regal gravitas remarkably well, especially through the mandra exploration and proper andolans on ga and dha in the intro. To tighten authenticity, eliminate the direct d-n-P stepwise motions in the mukhada and taan in favor of Darbari's signature vakra descent S'-d-n-P. Giving the first half of the pakad (S R g R g m P) a direct quotation in the mukhada would anchor the piece completely.","suggestions":[{"section":1,"change":"Replace 'd<>n d<>n n[1] P P' with 'm P d n P[1] m P'","why":"Darbari forbids a blunt upward run across dha into pa; komal dha must resolve vakra via 'd n P' to maintain the Kanada idiom."},{"section":5,"change":"Alter 'S(+1) d d n n P' to 'S(+1) d~[1] n P'","why":"Descending from taar Sa into the uttarang requires the characteristic vakra swoop onto oscillated komal dha swinging into n P, rather than straight scalar doubling."}]}
